A||||13||fiero56||legal action about bad checks||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||10-11-2001||06:26 PM||||The time is fast approaching. I am tired of the "I need to get to the bank" or "payroll sent my pay-check to the wrong office" or other such non-sense. <BR>I received over $1,400 worth of bad checks from a friend(I've seen $300 back so far, almost enough to cover MY bank fees for cashing bad checks). I have known them for 9 years, and we have been good friends, and I haven't wanted money to get between us, so I've been letting it slide. But it has been almost 8 weeks since I received the first notice from my bank saying the account the checks were from was closed(I didn't realize until then because I never check my acct balances).  By then I had accepted 4 checks from this person(its a long story, they don't believe in debit cards so I cashed a few checks for them to my account and gave them cash). <P>Its approaching 60 days, have I lost any time I had to get my money back through legal action? <P>They are still promising to give me the cash once they get to the bank, but I've been hearing this for almost 2 weeks. I'm through with talk, its time for action!<P>(Edited because I realized I've gotten $300 back so far).<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-11-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||SteveJ||10-11-2001||06:59 PM||||I am suprised you took the second check.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||10-11-2001||07:05 PM||||Supposedly a check is good for 6 mos.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||10-11-2001||07:08 PM||||It's a felony to write a check on a closed account! No you haven't waited too long. Statute of limitations is seven years.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||10-11-2001||07:20 PM||||With friends like that you don't need enemies!||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||10-11-2001||07:41 PM||||Someone that use to be a friend wrote bad checks to numerous businesses to get cash back.  The money was used to buy crack.<P>It is unbelievable how easy it is to cash checks.  If i ever own my own business, i will never take checks.<P>You probably can call the police on them.  I think thats what business usually do, or call a service to get on their ass to pay up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||10-11-2001||08:06 PM||||I never accept checks from anyone except major companies. If I dont get an insurance company or auto dealer check, they get no car except with cash. Friends who are not really friends dont pay if they can get away with it for a long time if ever. Laws vary from every juristiction. Youll have to check to see what you can do. Maybe you could have them arrested, or maybe you could file a claim in small claims court where you could get a judgement for amount owed plus costs of collecting it. When you (if) get your money get a new friend too. They will just keep taking advantage anyway they can. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||LarryB||10-11-2001||09:17 PM||||Sounds like you need to consider if you'd rather have a friend or your money.  At this point it's not going to be easy to have both. At the least, you'll probably never be able to look at these people the same. <P>Consider this a sad and painful lesson learned; think twice before loaning family and friends serious money with the expectation of getting it all back.  It's funny how some people change when $$$ is involved. <P>You can move against them legally, but if they don't have a proverbial pot to you-know-what in you may get little more than the satisfaction of feeling you were in the right.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||10-12-2001||05:43 AM||||I hate checks.  I went to a no check policy at my store.  I kept getting bounced checks back every week, even from some customers that had a great check cashing history.  My wife and I still have checks, but we use our debit card any time we can.  Little chance of a foul up that way and more businesses accept debit/credit cards than do checks.  <P>Drew--You still have time to go after this "friend" for your money.  If this person is really a friend, they'll pay up.  I've known people who claimed they were my friends stiff me for money a few times.  It just doesn't make you feel good no matter what happens.  If your "friend" wants to fix this problem, I'd get it in writing.  To hell with friendship.  Get your money.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick's Dad||10-12-2001||09:18 PM||||"Uttering False Instruments" is a felony. A check is a signed contract between you and the writer. For someone to knowingly write a check on a closed account is breach of contract before the contract was even negotiated.<P>OTOH, if this guy's a real friend, you may have to nudge him (I'd be emarrassed to talk to you - as a friend -  if I'd accidentally stiffed you like that) to get him to move. Be careful here, as, if this was done knowingly, you may have thin air instead of this "friend." It's more expensive to locate and prosecute than just to prosecute....||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||10-13-2001||07:57 AM||||Laws vary by state but most places allow  quite a while for charges to actually be filed. You might call the local police and ask just to be see how long you do have. If they knowingly wrote against a closed account that is usually more serious than just screwing up their balance. They may do jail time. <P>You will probably have a better chance of getting your money sooner by talking to them and avoiding filing charges if at all possible. The wheels of justice turn slow, plus they can't pay you from jail. <P>The squeaky wheel gets the grease... Become that wheel.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||10-13-2001||10:37 AM||||Drew--If this is a forum member, either past, present or future, you might want to let us know who it is so that we don't accept his checks, either.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||10-13-2001||01:06 PM||||Nope, its not a Forum member. I'd be putting out warnings if it were.<P>As far as I know, they didn't know the account was closed. Since they opened a new account with the same bank, supposedly the good and bad checkbooks are identical, and they missed destroying a bad one. <P>I'll be nudging more strongly very soon.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierospeeder||10-13-2001||03:19 PM||||ask them to give you a new check and see if the numbers on the bottom match the old checks.<P>Usually the first set of numbers is the routing number of the bank, the 2nd is the account number, and the 3rd is the check number.||||reg||1
